.SH "Install"
.PP
Install with \fRnpm\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
$ npm install \-\-save micromatch
.fi
.PP

.PP

.br
.SH "Sponsors"
.PP
\fRBecome a Sponsor\fP to add your logo to this README, or any of \fRmy other projects\fP

.PP

.br
.SS "Quickstart"
.PP
.nf
const micromatch = require('micromatch');
// micromatch(list, patterns[, options]);
.fi
.PP

.PP
The \fBmain export\fP takes a list of strings and one or more glob patterns:

.PP
.PP
.nf
console\&.log(micromatch(['foo', 'bar', 'baz', 'qux'], ['f*', 'b*'])) //=> ['foo', 'bar', 'baz']
console\&.log(micromatch(['foo', 'bar', 'baz', 'qux'], ['*', '!b*'])) //=> ['foo', 'qux']
.fi
.PP

.PP
Use \fB\&.isMatch()\fP to for boolean matching:

.PP
.PP
.nf
console\&.log(micromatch\&.isMatch('foo', 'f*')) //=> true
console\&.log(micromatch\&.isMatch('foo', ['b*', 'f*'])) //=> true
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fBSwitching\fP from minimatch and multimatch is easy!

.PP

.br
.SS "Why use micromatch?"
.RS 4
micromatch is a \fBreplacement\fP for minimatch and multimatch 
.RE
.PP

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Supports all of the same matching features as \fRminimatch\fP and \fRmultimatch\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
More complete support for the Bash 4\&.3 specification than minimatch and multimatch\&. Micromatch passes \fIall of the spec tests\fP from bash, including some that bash still fails\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBFast & Performant\fP - Loads in about 5ms and performs \fBfast matches\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBGlob matching\fP - Using wildcards (\fR*\fP and \fR?\fP), globstars (\fR**\fP) for nested directories
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB\fBAdvanced globbing\fP\fP - Supports \fBextglobs\fP, \fBbraces\fP, and \fBPOSIX brackets\fP, and support for escaping special characters with \fR\\\fP or quotes\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBAccurate\fP - Covers more scenarios \fRthan minimatch\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBWell tested\fP - More than 5,000 \fRtest assertions\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBWindows support\fP - More reliable windows support than minimatch and multimatch\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fB\fRSafe\fP\fP - Micromatch is not subject to DoS with brace patterns like minimatch and multimatch\&.
.PP
.SS "Matching features"
.IP "\(bu" 2
Support for multiple glob patterns (no need for wrappers like multimatch)
.IP "\(bu" 2
Wildcards (\fR**\fP, \fR*\&.js\fP)
.IP "\(bu" 2
Negation (\fR'!a/*\&.js'\fP, \fR'*!(b)\&.js'\fP)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBextglobs\fP (\fR+(x|y)\fP, \fR!(a|b)\fP)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fBPOSIX character classes\fP (\fR[[:alpha:][:digit:]]\fP)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRbrace expansion\fP (\fRfoo/{1\&.\&.5}\&.md\fP, \fRbar/{a,b,c}\&.js\fP)
.IP "\(bu" 2
regex character classes (\fRfoo-[1-5]\&.js\fP)
.IP "\(bu" 2
regex logical "or" (\fRfoo/(abc|xyz)\&.js\fP)
.PP

.PP
You can mix and match these features to create whatever patterns you need!
.SS "Switching to micromatch"
\fI(There is one notable difference between micromatch and minimatch in regards to how backslashes are handled\&. See \fBthe notes about backslashes\fP for more information\&.)\fP
.SS "From minimatch"
Use \fBmicromatch\&.isMatch()\fP instead of \fRminimatch()\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
console\&.log(micromatch\&.isMatch('foo', 'b*')); //=> false
.fi
.PP

.PP
Use \fBmicromatch\&.match()\fP instead of \fRminimatch\&.match()\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
console\&.log(micromatch\&.match(['foo', 'bar'], 'b*')); //=> 'bar'
.fi
.PP
.SS "From multimatch"
Same signature:

.PP
.PP
.nf
console\&.log(micromatch(['foo', 'bar', 'baz'], ['f*', '*z'])); //=> ['foo', 'baz']
.fi
.PP
.SS "API"
\fBParams\fP

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRlist\fP **{String|Array<string>}**: List of strings to match\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpatterns\fP **{String|Array<string>}**: One or more glob patterns to use for matching\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRoptions\fP **{Object}**: See available \fBoptions\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRreturns\fP **{Array}**: Returns an array of matches
.PP

.PP
\fBExample\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const mm = require('micromatch');
// mm(list, patterns[, options]);

console\&.log(mm(['a\&.js', 'a\&.txt'], ['*\&.js']));
//=> [ 'a\&.js' ]
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fR\&.matcher\fP"
Returns a matcher function from the given glob \fRpattern\fP and \fRoptions\fP\&. The returned function takes a string to match as its only argument and returns true if the string is a match\&.

.PP
\fBParams\fP

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpattern\fP **{String}**: Glob pattern
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRoptions\fP **{Object}**
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRreturns\fP **{Function}**: Returns a matcher function\&.
.PP

.PP
\fBExample\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const mm = require('micromatch');
// mm\&.matcher(pattern[, options]);

const isMatch = mm\&.matcher('*\&.!(*a)');
console\&.log(isMatch('a\&.a')); //=> false
console\&.log(isMatch('a\&.b')); //=> true
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fR\&.isMatch\fP"
Returns true if \fBany\fP of the given glob \fRpatterns\fP match the specified \fRstring\fP\&.

.PP
\fBParams\fP

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstr\fP **{String}**: The string to test\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpatterns\fP **{String|Array}**: One or more glob patterns to use for matching\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR[options]\fP **{Object}**: See available \fBoptions\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRreturns\fP **{Boolean}**: Returns true if any patterns match \fRstr\fP
.PP

.PP
\fBExample\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const mm = require('micromatch');
// mm\&.isMatch(string, patterns[, options]);

console\&.log(mm\&.isMatch('a\&.a', ['b\&.*', '*\&.a'])); //=> true
console\&.log(mm\&.isMatch('a\&.a', 'b\&.*')); //=> false
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fR\&.not\fP"
Returns a list of strings that \fI\fBdo not match any\fP\fP of the given \fRpatterns\fP\&.

.PP
\fBParams\fP

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRlist\fP **{Array}**: Array of strings to match\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpatterns\fP **{String|Array}**: One or more glob pattern to use for matching\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRoptions\fP **{Object}**: See available \fBoptions\fP for changing how matches are performed
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRreturns\fP **{Array}**: Returns an array of strings that \fBdo not match\fP the given patterns\&.
.PP

.PP
\fBExample\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const mm = require('micromatch');
// mm\&.not(list, patterns[, options]);

console\&.log(mm\&.not(['a\&.a', 'b\&.b', 'c\&.c'], '*\&.a'));
//=> ['b\&.b', 'c\&.c']
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fR\&.contains\fP"
Returns true if the given \fRstring\fP contains the given pattern\&. Similar to \fB\&.isMatch\fP but the pattern can match any part of the string\&.

.PP
\fBParams\fP

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstr\fP **{String}**: The string to match\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpatterns\fP **{String|Array}**: Glob pattern to use for matching\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRoptions\fP **{Object}**: See available \fBoptions\fP for changing how matches are performed
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRreturns\fP **{Boolean}**: Returns true if any of the patterns matches any part of \fRstr\fP\&.
.PP

.PP
\fBExample\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
var mm = require('micromatch');
// mm\&.contains(string, pattern[, options]);

console\&.log(mm\&.contains('aa/bb/cc', '*b'));
//=> true
console\&.log(mm\&.contains('aa/bb/cc', '*d'));
//=> false
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fR\&.matchKeys\fP"
Filter the keys of the given object with the given \fRglob\fP pattern and \fRoptions\fP\&. Does not attempt to match nested keys\&. If you need this feature, use \fRglob-object\fP instead\&.

.PP
\fBParams\fP

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRobject\fP **{Object}**: The object with keys to filter\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpatterns\fP **{String|Array}**: One or more glob patterns to use for matching\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRoptions\fP **{Object}**: See available \fBoptions\fP for changing how matches are performed
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRreturns\fP **{Object}**: Returns an object with only keys that match the given patterns\&.
.PP

.PP
\fBExample\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const mm = require('micromatch');
// mm\&.matchKeys(object, patterns[, options]);

const obj = { aa: 'a', ab: 'b', ac: 'c' };
console\&.log(mm\&.matchKeys(obj, '*b'));
//=> { ab: 'b' }
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fR\&.some\fP"
Returns true if some of the strings in the given \fRlist\fP match any of the given glob \fRpatterns\fP\&.

.PP
\fBParams\fP

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRlist\fP **{String|Array}**: The string or array of strings to test\&. Returns as soon as the first match is found\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpatterns\fP **{String|Array}**: One or more glob patterns to use for matching\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRoptions\fP **{Object}**: See available \fBoptions\fP for changing how matches are performed
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRreturns\fP **{Boolean}**: Returns true if any \fRpatterns\fP matches any of the strings in \fRlist\fP
.PP

.PP
\fBExample\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const mm = require('micromatch');
// mm\&.some(list, patterns[, options]);

console\&.log(mm\&.some(['foo\&.js', 'bar\&.js'], ['*\&.js', '!foo\&.js']));
// true
console\&.log(mm\&.some(['foo\&.js'], ['*\&.js', '!foo\&.js']));
// false
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fR\&.every\fP"
Returns true if every string in the given \fRlist\fP matches any of the given glob \fRpatterns\fP\&.

.PP
\fBParams\fP

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRlist\fP **{String|Array}**: The string or array of strings to test\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpatterns\fP **{String|Array}**: One or more glob patterns to use for matching\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRoptions\fP **{Object}**: See available \fBoptions\fP for changing how matches are performed
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRreturns\fP **{Boolean}**: Returns true if all \fRpatterns\fP matches all of the strings in \fRlist\fP
.PP

.PP
\fBExample\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const mm = require('micromatch');
// mm\&.every(list, patterns[, options]);

console\&.log(mm\&.every('foo\&.js', ['foo\&.js']));
// true
console\&.log(mm\&.every(['foo\&.js', 'bar\&.js'], ['*\&.js']));
// true
console\&.log(mm\&.every(['foo\&.js', 'bar\&.js'], ['*\&.js', '!foo\&.js']));
// false
console\&.log(mm\&.every(['foo\&.js'], ['*\&.js', '!foo\&.js']));
// false
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fR\&.all\fP"
Returns true if \fBall\fP of the given \fRpatterns\fP match the specified string\&.

.PP
\fBParams\fP

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRstr\fP **{String|Array}**: The string to test\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpatterns\fP **{String|Array}**: One or more glob patterns to use for matching\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRoptions\fP **{Object}**: See available \fBoptions\fP for changing how matches are performed
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRreturns\fP **{Boolean}**: Returns true if any patterns match \fRstr\fP
.PP

.PP
\fBExample\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const mm = require('micromatch');
// mm\&.all(string, patterns[, options]);

console\&.log(mm\&.all('foo\&.js', ['foo\&.js']));
// true

console\&.log(mm\&.all('foo\&.js', ['*\&.js', '!foo\&.js']));
// false

console\&.log(mm\&.all('foo\&.js', ['*\&.js', 'foo\&.js']));
// true

console\&.log(mm\&.all('foo\&.js', ['*\&.js', 'f*', '*o*', '*o\&.js']));
// true
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fR\&.capture\fP"
Returns an array of matches captured by \fRpattern\fP in \fRstring, or\fPnull` if the pattern did not match\&.

.PP
\fBParams\fP

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRglob\fP **{String}**: Glob pattern to use for matching\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRinput\fP **{String}**: String to match
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRoptions\fP **{Object}**: See available \fBoptions\fP for changing how matches are performed
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRreturns\fP **{Array|null}**: Returns an array of captures if the input matches the glob pattern, otherwise \fRnull\fP\&.
.PP

.PP
\fBExample\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const mm = require('micromatch');
// mm\&.capture(pattern, string[, options]);

console\&.log(mm\&.capture('test/*\&.js', 'test/foo\&.js'));
//=> ['foo']
console\&.log(mm\&.capture('test/*\&.js', 'foo/bar\&.css'));
//=> null
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fR\&.makeRe\fP"
Create a regular expression from the given glob \fRpattern\fP\&.

.PP
\fBParams\fP

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpattern\fP **{String}**: A glob pattern to convert to regex\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRoptions\fP **{Object}**
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRreturns\fP **{RegExp}**: Returns a regex created from the given pattern\&.
.PP

.PP
\fBExample\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const mm = require('micromatch');
// mm\&.makeRe(pattern[, options]);

console\&.log(mm\&.makeRe('*\&.js'));
//=> /^(?:(\\\&.[\\\\\\/])?(?!\\\&.)(?=\&.)[^\\/]*?\\\&.js)$/
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fR\&.scan\fP"
Scan a glob pattern to separate the pattern into segments\&. Used by the \fBsplit\fP method\&.

.PP
\fBParams\fP

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpattern\fP **{String}**
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRoptions\fP **{Object}**
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRreturns\fP **{Object}**: Returns an object with
.PP

.PP
\fBExample\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const mm = require('micromatch');
const state = mm\&.scan(pattern[, options]);
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fR\&.parse\fP"
Parse a glob pattern to create the source string for a regular expression\&.

.PP
\fBParams\fP

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRglob\fP **{String}**
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRoptions\fP **{Object}**
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRreturns\fP **{Object}**: Returns an object with useful properties and output to be used as regex source string\&.
.PP

.PP
\fBExample\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const mm = require('micromatch');
const state = mm\&.parse(pattern[, options]);
.fi
.PP
.SS "\fR\&.braces\fP"
Process the given brace \fRpattern\fP\&.

.PP
\fBParams\fP

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpattern\fP **{String}**: String with brace pattern to process\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRoptions\fP **{Object}**: Any \fBoptions\fP to change how expansion is performed\&. See the \fRbraces\fP library for all available options\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRreturns\fP **{Array}**
.PP

.PP
\fBExample\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const { braces } = require('micromatch');
console\&.log(braces('foo/{a,b,c}/bar'));
//=> [ 'foo/(a|b|c)/bar' ]

console\&.log(braces('foo/{a,b,c}/bar', { expand: true }));
//=> [ 'foo/a/bar', 'foo/b/bar', 'foo/c/bar' ]
.fi
.PP
.SS "Options"
\fBOption\fP  \fBType\fP  \fBDefault value\fP  \fBDescription\fP  \fRbasename\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRfalse\fP  If set, then patterns without slashes will be matched against the basename of the path if it contains slashes\&. For example, \fRa?b\fP would match the path \fR/xyz/123/acb\fP, but not \fR/xyz/acb/123\fP\&.  \fRbash\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRfalse\fP  Follow bash matching rules more strictly - disallows backslashes as escape characters, and treats single stars as globstars (\fR**\fP)\&.  \fRcapture\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRundefined\fP  Return regex matches in supporting methods\&.  \fRcontains\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRundefined\fP  Allows glob to match any part of the given string(s)\&.  \fRcwd\fP  \fRstring\fP  \fRprocess\&.cwd()\fP  Current working directory\&. Used by \fRpicomatch\&.split()\fP  \fRdebug\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRundefined\fP  Debug regular expressions when an error is thrown\&.  \fRdot\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRfalse\fP  Match dotfiles\&. Otherwise dotfiles are ignored unless a \fR\&.\fP is explicitly defined in the pattern\&.  \fRexpandRange\fP  \fRfunction\fP  \fRundefined\fP  Custom function for expanding ranges in brace patterns, such as \fR{a\&.\&.z}\fP\&. The function receives the range values as two arguments, and it must return a string to be used in the generated regex\&. It's recommended that returned strings be wrapped in parentheses\&. This option is overridden by the \fRexpandBrace\fP option\&.  \fRfailglob\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRfalse\fP  Similar to the \fRfailglob\fP behavior in Bash, throws an error when no matches are found\&. Based on the bash option of the same name\&.  \fRfastpaths\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRtrue\fP  To speed up processing, full parsing is skipped for a handful common glob patterns\&. Disable this behavior by setting this option to \fRfalse\fP\&.  \fRflags\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRundefined\fP  Regex flags to use in the generated regex\&. If defined, the \fRnocase\fP option will be overridden\&.  \fBformat\fP  \fRfunction\fP  \fRundefined\fP  Custom function for formatting the returned string\&. This is useful for removing leading slashes, converting Windows paths to Posix paths, etc\&.  \fRignore\fP  \fRarray\\|string\fP  \fRundefined\fP  One or more glob patterns for excluding strings that should not be matched from the result\&.  \fRkeepQuotes\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRfalse\fP  Retain quotes in the generated regex, since quotes may also be used as an alternative to backslashes\&.  \fRliteralBrackets\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRundefined\fP  When \fRtrue\fP, brackets in the glob pattern will be escaped so that only literal brackets will be matched\&.  \fRlookbehinds\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRtrue\fP  Support regex positive and negative lookbehinds\&. Note that you must be using Node 8\&.1\&.10 or higher to enable regex lookbehinds\&.  \fRmatchBase\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRfalse\fP  Alias for \fRbasename\fP  \fRmaxLength\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fR65536\fP  Limit the max length of the input string\&. An error is thrown if the input string is longer than this value\&.  \fRnobrace\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRfalse\fP  Disable brace matching, so that \fR{a,b}\fP and \fR{1\&.\&.3}\fP would be treated as literal characters\&.  \fRnobracket\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRundefined\fP  Disable matching with regex brackets\&.  \fRnocase\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRfalse\fP  Perform case-insensitive matching\&. Equivalent to the regex \fRi\fP flag\&. Note that this option is ignored when the \fRflags\fP option is defined\&.  \fRnodupes\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRtrue\fP  Deprecated, use \fRnounique\fP instead\&. This option will be removed in a future major release\&. By default duplicates are removed\&. Disable uniquification by setting this option to false\&.  \fRnoext\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRfalse\fP  Alias for \fRnoextglob\fP  \fRnoextglob\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRfalse\fP  Disable support for matching with \fBextglobs\fP (like \fR+(a\\|b)\fP)  \fRnoglobstar\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRfalse\fP  Disable support for matching nested directories with globstars (\fR**\fP)  \fRnonegate\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRfalse\fP  Disable support for negating with leading \fR!\fP  \fRnoquantifiers\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRfalse\fP  Disable support for regex quantifiers (like \fRa{1,2}\fP) and treat them as brace patterns to be expanded\&.  \fBonIgnore\fP  \fRfunction\fP  \fRundefined\fP  Function to be called on ignored items\&.  \fBonMatch\fP  \fRfunction\fP  \fRundefined\fP  Function to be called on matched items\&.  \fBonResult\fP  \fRfunction\fP  \fRundefined\fP  Function to be called on all items, regardless of whether or not they are matched or ignored\&.  \fRposix\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRfalse\fP  Support \fBPOSIX character classes\fP ("posix brackets")\&.  \fRposixSlashes\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRundefined\fP  Convert all slashes in file paths to forward slashes\&. This does not convert slashes in the glob pattern itself  \fRprepend\fP  \fRstring\fP  \fRundefined\fP  String to prepend to the generated regex used for matching\&.  \fRregex\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRfalse\fP  Use regular expression rules for \fR+\fP (instead of matching literal \fR+\fP), and for stars that follow closing parentheses or brackets (as in \fR)*\fP and \fR]*\fP)\&.  \fRstrictBrackets\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRundefined\fP  Throw an error if brackets, braces, or parens are imbalanced\&.  \fRstrictSlashes\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRundefined\fP  When true, picomatch won't match trailing slashes with single stars\&.  \fRunescape\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRundefined\fP  Remove preceding backslashes from escaped glob characters before creating the regular expression to perform matches\&.  \fRunixify\fP  \fRboolean\fP  \fRundefined\fP  Alias for \fRposixSlashes\fP, for backwards compatitibility\&.  
.SS "Options Examples"
.SS "options\&.basename"
Allow glob patterns without slashes to match a file path based on its basename\&. Same behavior as \fRminimatch\fP option \fRmatchBase\fP\&.

.PP
\fBType\fP: \fRBoolean\fP

.PP
\fBDefault\fP: \fRfalse\fP

.PP
\fBExample\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
micromatch(['a/b\&.js', 'a/c\&.md'], '*\&.js');
//=> []

micromatch(['a/b\&.js', 'a/c\&.md'], '*\&.js', { basename: true });
//=> ['a/b\&.js']
.fi
.PP
.SS "options\&.bash"
Enabled by default, this option enforces bash-like behavior with stars immediately following a bracket expression\&. Bash bracket expressions are similar to regex character classes, but unlike regex, a star following a bracket expression \fBdoes not repeat the bracketed characters\fP\&. Instead, the star is treated the same as any other star\&.

.PP
\fBType\fP: \fRBoolean\fP

.PP
\fBDefault\fP: \fRtrue\fP

.PP
\fBExample\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const files = ['abc', 'ajz'];
console\&.log(micromatch(files, '[a\-c]*'));
//=> ['abc', 'ajz']

console\&.log(micromatch(files, '[a\-c]*', { bash: false }));
.fi
.PP
.SS "options\&.expandRange"
\fBType\fP: \fRfunction\fP

.PP
\fBDefault\fP: \fRundefined\fP

.PP
Custom function for expanding ranges in brace patterns\&. The \fRfill-range\fP library is ideal for this purpose, or you can use custom code to do whatever you need\&.

.PP
\fBExample\fP

.PP
The following example shows how to create a glob that matches a numeric folder name between \fR01\fP and \fR25\fP, with leading zeros\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
const fill = require('fill\-range');
const regex = micromatch\&.makeRe('foo/{01\&.\&.25}/bar', {
  expandRange(a, b) {
    return `(${fill(a, b, { toRegex: true })})`;
  }
});

console\&.log(regex)
//=> /^(?:foo\\/((?:0[1\-9]|1[0\-9]|2[0\-5]))\\/bar)$/

console\&.log(regex\&.test('foo/00/bar')) // false
console\&.log(regex\&.test('foo/01/bar')) // true
console\&.log(regex\&.test('foo/10/bar')) // true
console\&.log(regex\&.test('foo/22/bar')) // true
console\&.log(regex\&.test('foo/25/bar')) // true
console\&.log(regex\&.test('foo/26/bar')) // false
.fi
.PP
.SS "options\&.format"
\fBType\fP: \fRfunction\fP

.PP
\fBDefault\fP: \fRundefined\fP

.PP
Custom function for formatting strings before they're matched\&.

.PP
\fBExample\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
// strip leading '\&./' from strings
const format = str => str\&.replace(/^\\\&.\\//, '');
const isMatch = picomatch('foo/*\&.js', { format });
console\&.log(isMatch('\&./foo/bar\&.js')) //=> true
.fi
.PP
.SS "options\&.ignore"
String or array of glob patterns to match files to ignore\&.

.PP
\fBType\fP: \fRString|Array\fP

.PP
\fBDefault\fP: \fRundefined\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
const isMatch = micromatch\&.matcher('*', { ignore: 'f*' });
console\&.log(isMatch('foo')) //=> false
console\&.log(isMatch('bar')) //=> true
console\&.log(isMatch('baz')) //=> true
.fi
.PP
.SS "options\&.matchBase"
Alias for \fBoptions\&.basename\fP\&.
.SS "options\&.noextglob"
Disable extglob support, so that \fBextglobs\fP are regarded as literal characters\&.

.PP
\fBType\fP: \fRBoolean\fP

.PP
\fBDefault\fP: \fRundefined\fP

.PP
\fBExamples\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
console\&.log(micromatch(['a/z', 'a/b', 'a/!(z)'], 'a/!(z)'));
//=> ['a/b', 'a/!(z)']

console\&.log(micromatch(['a/z', 'a/b', 'a/!(z)'], 'a/!(z)', { noextglob: true }));
//=> ['a/!(z)'] (matches only as literal characters)
.fi
.PP
.SS "options\&.nonegate"
Disallow negation (\fR!\fP) patterns, and treat leading \fR!\fP as a literal character to match\&.

.PP
\fBType\fP: \fRBoolean\fP

.PP
\fBDefault\fP: \fRundefined\fP
.SS "options\&.noglobstar"
Disable matching with globstars (\fR**\fP)\&.

.PP
\fBType\fP: \fRBoolean\fP

.PP
\fBDefault\fP: \fRundefined\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
micromatch(['a/b', 'a/b/c', 'a/b/c/d'], 'a/**');
//=> ['a/b', 'a/b/c', 'a/b/c/d']

micromatch(['a/b', 'a/b/c', 'a/b/c/d'], 'a/**', {noglobstar: true});
//=> ['a/b']
.fi
.PP
.SS "options\&.nonull"
Alias for \fBoptions\&.nullglob\fP\&.
.SS "options\&.nullglob"
If \fRtrue\fP, when no matches are found the actual (arrayified) glob pattern is returned instead of an empty array\&. Same behavior as \fRminimatch\fP option \fRnonull\fP\&.

.PP
\fBType\fP: \fRBoolean\fP

.PP
\fBDefault\fP: \fRundefined\fP
.SS "options\&.onIgnore"
.PP
.nf
const onIgnore = ({ glob, regex, input, output }) => {
  console\&.log({ glob, regex, input, output });
  // { glob: '*', regex: /^(?:(?!\\\&.)(?=\&.)[^\\/]*?\\/?)$/, input: 'foo', output: 'foo' }
};

const isMatch = micromatch\&.matcher('*', { onIgnore, ignore: 'f*' });
isMatch('foo');
isMatch('bar');
isMatch('baz');
.fi
.PP
.SS "options\&.onMatch"
.PP
.nf
const onMatch = ({ glob, regex, input, output }) => {
  console\&.log({ input, output });
  // { input: 'some\\\\path', output: 'some/path' }
  // { input: 'some\\\\path', output: 'some/path' }
  // { input: 'some\\\\path', output: 'some/path' }
};

const isMatch = micromatch\&.matcher('**', { onMatch, posixSlashes: true });
isMatch('some\\\\path');
isMatch('some\\\\path');
isMatch('some\\\\path');
.fi
.PP
.SS "options\&.onResult"
.PP
.nf
const onResult = ({ glob, regex, input, output }) => {
  console\&.log({ glob, regex, input, output });
};

const isMatch = micromatch('*', { onResult, ignore: 'f*' });
isMatch('foo');
isMatch('bar');
isMatch('baz');
.fi
.PP
.SS "options\&.posixSlashes"
Convert path separators on returned files to posix/unix-style forward slashes\&. Aliased as \fRunixify\fP for backwards compatibility\&.

.PP
\fBType\fP: \fRBoolean\fP

.PP
\fBDefault\fP: \fRtrue\fP on windows, \fRfalse\fP everywhere else\&.

.PP
\fBExample\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
console\&.log(micromatch\&.match(['a\\\\b\\\\c'], 'a/**'));
//=> ['a/b/c']

console\&.log(micromatch\&.match(['a\\\\b\\\\c'], { posixSlashes: false }));
//=> ['a\\\\b\\\\c']
.fi
.PP
.SS "options\&.unescape"
Remove backslashes from escaped glob characters before creating the regular expression to perform matches\&.

.PP
\fBType\fP: \fRBoolean\fP

.PP
\fBDefault\fP: \fRundefined\fP

.PP
\fBExample\fP

.PP
In this example we want to match a literal \fR*\fP:

.PP
.PP
.nf
console\&.log(micromatch\&.match(['abc', 'a\\\\*c'], 'a\\\\*c'));
//=> ['a\\\\*c']

console\&.log(micromatch\&.match(['abc', 'a\\\\*c'], 'a\\\\*c', { unescape: true }));
//=> ['a*c']
.fi
.PP

.PP

.br
 
.br
.SS "Extended globbing"
Micromatch supports the following extended globbing features\&.
.SS "Extglobs"
Extended globbing, as described by the bash man page:

.PP
\fBpattern\fP  \fBregex equivalent\fP  \fBdescription\fP  \fR?(pattern)\fP  \fR(pattern)?\fP  Matches zero or one occurrence of the given patterns  \fR*(pattern)\fP  \fR(pattern)*\fP  Matches zero or more occurrences of the given patterns  \fR+(pattern)\fP  \fR(pattern)+\fP  Matches one or more occurrences of the given patterns  \fR@(pattern)\fP  \fR(pattern)\fP \*{*\*}   Matches one of the given patterns  \fR!(pattern)\fP  N/A (equivalent regex is much more complicated)  Matches anything except one of the given patterns  

.PP
\*{\fB*\fP\*}  Note that \fR@\fP isn't a regex character\&.
.SS "Braces"
Brace patterns can be used to match specific ranges or sets of characters\&.

.PP
\fBExample\fP

.PP
The pattern \fR{f,b}*/{1\&.\&.3}/{b,q}*\fP would match any of following strings:

.PP
.PP
.nf
foo/1/bar
foo/2/bar
foo/3/bar
baz/1/qux
baz/2/qux
baz/3/qux
.fi
.PP

.PP
Visit \fRbraces\fP to see the full range of features and options related to brace expansion, or to create brace matching or expansion related issues\&.
.SS "Regex character classes"
Given the list: \fR['a\&.js', 'b\&.js', 'c\&.js', 'd\&.js', 'E\&.js']\fP:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR[ac]\&.js\fP: matches both \fRa\fP and \fRc\fP, returning \fR['a\&.js', 'c\&.js']\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR[b-d]\&.js\fP: matches from \fRb\fP to \fRd\fP, returning \fR['b\&.js', 'c\&.js', 'd\&.js']\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRa/[A-Z]\&.js\fP: matches and uppercase letter, returning \fR['a/E\&.md']\fP
.PP

.PP
Learn about \fRregex character classes\fP\&.
.SS "Regex groups"
Given \fR['a\&.js', 'b\&.js', 'c\&.js', 'd\&.js', 'E\&.js']\fP:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR(a|c)\&.js\fP: would match either \fRa\fP or \fRc\fP, returning \fR['a\&.js', 'c\&.js']\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR(b|d)\&.js\fP: would match either \fRb\fP or \fRd\fP, returning \fR['b\&.js', 'd\&.js']\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fR(b|[A-Z])\&.js\fP: would match either \fRb\fP or an uppercase letter, returning \fR['b\&.js', 'E\&.js']\fP
.PP

.PP
As with regex, parens can be nested, so patterns like \fR((a|b)|c)/b\fP will work\&. Although brace expansion might be friendlier to use, depending on preference\&.
.SS "POSIX bracket expressions"
POSIX brackets are intended to be more user-friendly than regex character classes\&. This of course is in the eye of the beholder\&.

.PP
\fBExample\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
console\&.log(micromatch\&.isMatch('a1', '[[:alpha:][:digit:]]')) //=> true
console\&.log(micromatch\&.isMatch('a1', '[[:alpha:][:alpha:]]')) //=> false
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.SS "Notes"
.SS "Bash 4\&.3 parity"
Whenever possible matching behavior is based on behavior Bash 4\&.3, which is mostly consistent with minimatch\&.

.PP
However, it's suprising how many edge cases and rabbit holes there are with glob matching, and since there is no real glob specification, and micromatch is more accurate than both Bash and minimatch, there are cases where best-guesses were made for behavior\&. In a few cases where Bash had no answers, we used wildmatch (used by git) as a fallback\&.
.SS "Backslashes"
There is an important, notable difference between minimatch and micromatch \fIin regards to how backslashes are handled\fP in glob patterns\&.

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Micromatch exclusively and explicitly reserves backslashes for escaping characters in a glob pattern, even on windows, which is consistent with bash behavior\&. \fIMore importantly, unescaping globs can result in unsafe regular expressions\fP\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Minimatch converts all backslashes to forward slashes, which means you can't use backslashes to escape any characters in your glob patterns\&.
.PP

.PP
We made this decision for micromatch for a couple of reasons:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
Consistency with bash conventions\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
Glob patterns are not filepaths\&. They are a type of \fRregular language\fP that is converted to a JavaScript regular expression\&. Thus, when forward slashes are defined in a glob pattern, the resulting regular expression will match windows or POSIX path separators just fine\&.
.PP

.PP
\fBA note about joining paths to globs\fP

.PP
Note that when you pass something like \fRpath\&.join('foo', '*')\fP to micromatch, you are creating a filepath and expecting it to still work as a glob pattern\&. This causes problems on windows, since the \fRpath\&.sep\fP is \fR\\\\\fP\&.

.PP
In other words, since \fR\\\\\fP is reserved as an escape character in globs, on windows \fRpath\&.join('foo', '*')\fP would result in \fRfoo\\\\*\fP, which tells micromatch to match \fR*\fP as a literal character\&. This is the same behavior as bash\&.

.PP
To solve this, you might be inspired to do something like \fR'foo\\\\*'\&.replace(/\\\\/g, '/')\fP, but this causes another, potentially much more serious, problem\&.
